---
layout: base.njk
bodyClass: docs-home
---
<main class="docs-home">
	<section class="docs-hero-panel">
		<div class="container">
			{#- .container is Cirth's own 3-track grid (gutter/content/gutter),
			    keyed to --cirth-container-max-width. .docs-hero is a *different*
			    grid (single centered column). Combining both on one element
			    meant two competing display:grid/grid-template-columns
			    declarations fighting for the same box, with .docs-hero-copy and
			    .docs-hero-demo then implicitly auto-placed into whichever
			    grid-template-columns actually won — fragile, and the likely
			    cause of the hero rendering off-center in some browsers/
			    viewports. Nesting instead of combining removes the ambiguity:
			    .container centers its content track as designed, .docs-hero
			    governs its own children inside that track, no shared box. -#}
			{#- The hero is an identification plate: the wordmark, what the
			    thing is, and then the figures you'd read off a machine —
			    model, revision, weight, input. Every value comes from
			    _data/spec.js, which measures dist/ and reads package.json
			    at build time, so the plate can't drift out of date the way
			    a hand-typed number would. The exploded drawing sits beside
			    it, the way a parts diagram sits beside a spec table. -#}
			<div class="docs-hero">
				<div class="docs-hero-copy">
					<hgroup>
						<h1 class="docs-hero-title">
							<img class="docs-hero-mark docs-logo-light" src="/logo_brand.svg" alt="" width="56" height="56" />
							<img class="docs-hero-mark docs-logo-dark" src="/logo_brand_dark.svg" alt="" width="56" height="56" />
							<span>{{ hero.name }}</span>
						</h1>
						<p class="docs-hero-eyebrow">{{ hero.eyebrow }}</p>
					</hgroup>

					<dl class="docs-plate">
						<div>
							<dt>Revision</dt>
							<dd>{{ spec.version }}</dd>
						</div>
						<div>
							<dt>Weight</dt>
							<dd>{{ spec.defaultBuildLabel }} <span class="docs-plate-unit">gzipped</span></dd>
						</div>
						<div>
							<dt>Builds</dt>
							<dd>{{ spec.builds }} <span class="docs-plate-unit">variants</span></dd>
						</div>
						<div>
							<dt>Input</dt>
							<dd>Standard HTML</dd>
						</div>
					</dl>

					<p class="docs-hero-tagline">{{ hero.text }}</p>
					<p class="docs-hero-actions">
						{% for action in hero.actions %}
							<a href="{{ action.link }}" role="button"{% if action.theme != "brand" %} class="outline secondary"{% endif %}>{{ action.text }}</a>
						{% endfor %}
					</p>
				</div>
				{#- The live demo, beside the plate: plain semantic HTML is
				    typed on the left and the rendered result builds up on the
				    right, so the hero's claim ("write HTML, it's already
				    styled") is demonstrated rather than asserted. The full
				    final state is server-rendered inside the include, so
				    no-JS visitors and assistive tech get the complete markup;
				    the typing is a visual layer applied after load and
				    skipped entirely under prefers-reduced-motion. -#}
				<div class="docs-hero-figure">
					{% include "home-demo.njk" %}
					<p class="docs-hero-figure-caption">Fig. 1 — Input and output</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	{% if pitch %}
		<section class="docs-pitch">
			<div class="container">
				<header class="docs-section-header docs-reveal">
					<p class="docs-eyebrow">{{ pitch.eyebrow }}</p>
					<h2>{{ pitch.title }}</h2>
					<p class="docs-section-lede">{{ pitch.lede }}</p>
				</header>
				<div class="docs-pitch-grid">
					{% for card in pitch.items %}
						<article class="docs-reveal" style="transition-delay: {{ loop.index0 * 60 }}ms">
							<strong class="docs-pitch-value">{{ card.value }}</strong>
							<p>{{ card.details | safe }}</p>
						</article>
					{% endfor %}
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>
	{% endif %}

	<div class="container">
		{% if stack %}
			{#- Sticky card stack (like FastHTML's "scales up and down"
			    section): each card is position:sticky with an increasing
			    `top`, so as you scroll, every card pins in place and the
			    next one slides up to cover all but a sliver of it — pure
			    CSS, the browser's own scroll position drives it, nothing to
			    compute in JS. Falls back to plain stacked cards under
			    768px (see style.css) where the peek effect reads as
			    cramped rather than deliberate. -#}
			<section class="docs-stack">
				{#- position:sticky lives on a plain, unstyled wrapper div —
				    never directly on an element that also has a background
				    color, border-radius, or box-shadow. Safari/WebKit renders
				    a visible concentric-ring repaint artifact right as a
				    *styled* sticky element approaches its release point;
				    splitting "what's sticky" (this wrapper) from "what's
				    painted" (the header/card inside it) sidesteps the bug.
				    Same pattern below for each card.

				    The header's sticky wrapper additionally sits inside its
				    own short .docs-stack-header-zone (see style.css) rather
				    than spanning the whole section: a sticky element can only
				    stay "stuck" within its own parent's height, so a short
				    parent makes it release early — right after the first
				    card or so — instead of clinging to the top all the way
				    through and colliding with the *last* card's own release
				    at the very end of the section (that collision is what
				    made the lede line look detached, sliding away on its own
				    under the final card). Same idea as fastht.ml's header,
				    which releases via an explicit `bottom` offset — this is
				    a container-height version of the same trick. -#}
				<div class="docs-stack-header-zone">
					<div class="docs-stack-sticky">
						<header class="docs-section-header docs-reveal">
							<p class="docs-eyebrow">{{ stack.eyebrow }}</p>
							<h2>{{ stack.title }}</h2>
							<p class="docs-section-lede">{{ stack.lede | safe }}</p>
						</header>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="docs-stack-list">
					{% for card in stack.items %}
						<div class="docs-stack-sticky docs-stack-card-sticky" style="top: calc(2rem + {{ loop.index0 * 1.25 }}rem)">
							<article class="docs-stack-card">
								<span class="docs-stack-index">{{ "0" if loop.index < 10 }}{{ loop.index }}</span>
								<h3>{{ card.title }}</h3>
								<p>{{ card.details | safe }}</p>
								<ul class="docs-badge-row">
									{% for badge in card.badges %}<li><code>{{ badge }}</code></li>{% endfor %}
								</ul>
							</article>
						</div>
					{% endfor %}
				</div>
			</section>
		{% endif %}

		{% if showcase %}
			<section class="docs-showcase">
				<header class="docs-section-header docs-reveal">
					<p class="docs-eyebrow">{{ showcase.eyebrow }}</p>
					<h2>{{ showcase.title }}</h2>
					<p class="docs-section-lede">{{ showcase.lede }}</p>
				</header>
				<div class="docs-showcase-grid">
					{% for item in showcase.items %}
						<a class="docs-showcase-item docs-reveal" style="transition-delay: {{ loop.index0 * 70 }}ms" href="{{ item.link }}">
							<span class="docs-showcase-icon">{% icon item.icon, 28 %}</span>
							<strong>{{ item.title }}</strong>
							<span>{{ item.details }}</span>
						</a>
					{% endfor %}
				</div>
				<p class="docs-showcase-cta">
					<a href="{{ showcase.link }}" role="button" class="outline secondary">{{ showcase.linkText }}</a>
				</p>
			</section>
		{% endif %}

		{% if faq %}
			<section class="docs-faq docs-reveal">
				<header class="docs-section-header">
					<p class="docs-eyebrow">{{ faq.eyebrow }}</p>
					<h2>{{ faq.title }}</h2>
				</header>
				<div class="docs-faq-list">
					{% for item in faq.items %}
						<details>
							<summary>{{ item.q }}</summary>
							<p>{{ item.a | safe }}</p>
						</details>
					{% endfor %}
				</div>
			</section>
		{% endif %}
	</div>
</main>
